{"edits": [{"finding_ids": ["scripts/clean_backup.py:146:deprecated-method", "scripts/clean_backup.py:147:deprecated-method"], "search": "            User.parse_obj({"user_id": args.user_id, "user_type": args.user_type}),
            User.parse_obj(
                {
                    "user_id": args.replacement_user_id,
                    "user_type": args.replacement_user_type,
                }
            ),", "replace": "            User.model_validate({"user_id": args.user_id, "user_type": args.user_type}),
            User.model_validate(
                {
                    "user_id": args.replacement_user_id,
                    "user_type": args.replacement_user_type,
                }
            ),", "explanation": "Pydantic v2 deprecates BaseModel.parse_obj() in favor of model_validate() (migration.md, 'Changes to pydantic.BaseModel' table: parse_obj() -> model_validate()). Both calls build User instances from a dict with the same arguments, so behavior is unchanged. The two calls are in one contiguous block, so they are addressed in a single edit."}], "notes": "Both findings (lines 146 and 147) are the two `User.parse_obj(...)` calls in the `replace-user` branch. They are adjacent, so one edit replaces both with `User.model_validate(...)`, keeping the arguments and indentation identical. No import changes are needed because `User` is still imported and used. The `user.user_id = ...` assignments in replace_user are not part of any finding and are left alone. No other changes are required in this file."}
